  "cli_result_text": "Submitted the 13 source files required to trace the FastAPI \u2194 httpx contract-drift analysis:\n\n**FastAPI request validation chain:**\n- `fastapi/routing.py` \u2014 `get_request_handler`/`app()`: Content-Type negotiation (form vs JSON), reads `request.body()`/`request.json()`, catches `json.JSONDecodeError`\n- `fastapi/dependencies/utils.py` \u2014 `solve_dependencies`, `request_body_to_args`, `get_body_field`: maps parsed body dict onto Pydantic `ModelField`s, embed/alias logic\n- `fastapi/_compat.py` \u2014 Pydantic v1/v2 compat shim (`ModelField`, `_model_dump`, `field_annotation_is_scalar`) where actual validation happens\n- `fastapi/encoders.py` \u2014 `jsonable_encoder`, the None/default/nested-model serialization FastAPI itself uses on the way out (useful contrast for the drift analysis)\n- `fastapi/params.py`, `fastapi/exceptions.py`, `fastapi/exception_handlers.py` \u2014 `Body()` field definitions and `RequestValidationError`/422 formatting\n\n**httpx request encoding chain:**\n- `httpx/_content.py` \u2014 `encode_json`/`encode_request`: plain `json.dumps()` with no custom encoder (no datetime/UUID/Decimal support, no None-handling logic)\n- `httpx/_models.py` \u2014 `Request.__init__`: wires `json=`/`data=`/`content=` into `encode_request`, sets Content-Type/Content-Length\n- `httpx/_client.py` \u2014 `build_request`: public API surface where `json=` is accepted\n- `httpx/_api.py` \u2014 top-level `httpx.post()`/`request()` signatures\n- `httpx/_multipart.py`, `httpx/_types.py` \u2014 form/multipart encoding path and type aliases (`RequestData`, `RequestFiles`) for contrast with JSON path\n\nThese are enough to trace both chains end-to-end and identify the concrete drift points (e.g., httpx's `json_dumps(json)` has no `exclude_none`/custom-encoder support the way `jsonable_encoder` does, so a caller who does `httpx.post(url, json=model.dict())` without `exclude_none=True` sends explicit `null`s for Optional fields with defaults, which trips FastAPI/Pydantic v2 union validation differently than a missing key would).",
